(vector (map 
   (:db/id -1)
   (:adr/id "adr-2608271200-rfc-9180-hpke-program-suites-modes-and-consumers")
   (:adr/title "RFC 9180 HPKE: four modes landed, AES-GCM is the keystone for the rest")
   (:adr/status "accepted")
   (:adr/date "2026-08-27")
   (:adr/body "# RFC 9180 HPKE — the whole surface, and what it costs

Asked whether the wolfSSL article's HPKE was implemented here. It is:
`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-hpke`, RFC 9180, portable .cljc. The article enumerates
five KEMs, three KDFs, three AEADs and four modes; this had one of each KEM,
KDF and AEAD, and one mode. The owner then asked for all four gaps: the
remaining modes, the remaining suites, TLS-ECH, and ODoH/MLS.

## The measurement that reordered the work

The claim that stopped the other suites was written into org-ietf-hpke's own
docstring: \"AES-GCM, P-256, P-384, P-521, X448, SHA-384 and SHA-512 are each
an implementation this workspace does not have.\"

Two of those were wrong. `sha2.sha512` has had SHA-384 and SHA-512 since the
Ed25519 work, and `org-nist-sha2`'s README simultaneously documented them and
listed them under \"Not implemented\" — the stale sentence sat three headings
above the section describing the functions. Both files have been corrected.

The rest was measured rather than assumed, and holds:

  AES / AES-GCM     was absent. Every AES in the tree was Node's crypto
                    (kotobase's storage profile and erasure lifecycle).
                    GitHub code search reported nothing, but that index does
                    not cover this org -- controls for `hpke` and `x25519`,
                    both known present, also return zero, so the finding came
                    from reading trees. **Now kotoba-lang/org-nist-aes.**
  P-256/384/521     was absent -- btc-crypto has secp256k1 point arithmetic
                    but schnorr.cljc and core.cljc are #?(:clj) only over
                    java.math.BigInteger and the :cljs branch throws.
                    **Now kotoba-lang/org-nist-ecc.**
  X448 / Curve448   was absent entirely. **Now kotoba-lang/org-ietf-x448.**
  HMAC-SHA384       was missing. Added.

## What is done

**Four modes, anchored to the RFC rather than to an oracle.** RFC 9180
Appendix A.2 is this library's suite exactly -- DHKEM(X25519, HKDF-SHA256),
HKDF-SHA256, ChaCha20Poly1305 -- and publishes base, PSK, Auth and AuthPSK.
The earlier argument for shipping base alone was that the others would be
untested code paths; that was an argument about evidence, and the evidence
was in the specification.

A.2 is extracted by `scripts/extract_rfc9180.cljs` from a text pinned by
sha256, not transcribed. The repo had already been bitten once: an earlier
suite claimed A.2 vectors and carried A.1's ikmR, caught only by an
assertion on pkRm. 404 assertions on both runtimes, 591 with the oracle.

Discrimination, measured one break at a time: dropping the mode byte from
key_schedule_context turns 245 red across all four modes; swapping pkRm/pkSm
in the auth kem_context turns 64 red and leaves base and PSK **green**;
dropping auth-encap's second DH, the same 64. The localisation is the point
-- it is what makes those numbers evidence about the added code.


**HMAC-SHA-384**, for HKDF-SHA384. The substitution that matters is the key
transformation: HMAC-SHA-384 and HMAC-SHA-512 share a 128-byte block, so
using the wrong hash there changes nothing until a key exceeds 128 bytes.
RFC 4231 case 6 is that key, and it is the single assertion the break turns
red.

**org-nist-aes** -- AES (FIPS 197, forward cipher only) and AES-GCM (SP
800-38D), zero dependencies, seal/open mirroring chacha20.aead so an HPKE
suite can be a parameter rather than a rewrite. Tested against the complete
Wycheproof corpus: 316 cases, every group, no sampling, pinned by sha256 and
re-extractable. 1,368 assertions on both runtimes, 1,667 with a javax.crypto
oracle sweeping message, AAD and IV lengths the fixed corpus does not name.

Two rows of its break table are worth carrying here. Incrementing the whole
128-bit block instead of the low 32 bits turns **54** of 1,368 red -- the
break is invisible to any message short enough not to carry. Comparing only
the first tag byte turns **108** red and **only in the cases that are
supposed to fail**: every correct decryption stays correct, so a suite
without adversarial cases would have called it green.

And one that is not hypothetical: it shipped on the first run with `Rcon[j]`
written as `x^j` instead of `x^(j-1)`. The cipher was still a permutation,
still round-tripped against itself, and agreed with nothing else in the
world -- 866 assertions red. **Deriving a table protects against a mistyped
constant, not against an off-by-one in the index that reads it**, which is a
narrower guarantee than the derive-don't-transcribe habit implies.

**org-nist-ecc** -- P-256, P-384 and P-521 over Fp, Jacobian coordinates,
ECDH. One implementation, three parameter sets: all three have a = p - 3, so
the doubling formula specialised for a = -3 is not an optimisation chosen for
them, it is why they were given that a. Against the complete Wycheproof ECDH
corpus (1,806 cases, raw-point encoding, pinned by sha256) plus a
java.security oracle. 3,745 assertions on ClojureScript, 3,818 with the
oracle.

**org-ietf-x448** -- RFC 7748, the same shape as org-ietf-x25519. Against the
RFC's own vectors and the complete Wycheproof corpus (510 cases). 1,107
assertions on both runtimes.

**HPKE, fully parameterised** -- five KEMs, three KDFs, four AEADs including
export-only, four modes, against all seven RFC 9180 appendices. Each test
builds its suite from the identifiers the vectors themselves declare, so the
registries are checked too. 2,423 assertions on both runtimes, 2,610 with the
BouncyCastle oracle.

Appendix A.4 is the one that earns its keep: DHKEM(P-256, HKDF-SHA256) paired
with HKDF-SHA512, so the KEM derives with SHA-256 while the key schedule
derives with SHA-512 in the same suite. A library reading one KDF for both is
right about six appendices out of seven.

**tls.ech in org-ietf-tls** -- draft-ietf-tls-esni-25, the ECH data plane:
configurations, EncodedClientHelloInner and its reconstruction,
ClientHelloOuterAAD, seal and open, and the acceptance confirmation. Client
side and client-facing server side in one namespace, so the tests run both.
1,472 assertions on the JVM, 922 of them on ClojureScript.

Measured before starting, as this ADR asked: ECH is **not an RFC** -- the
datatracker gives draft-ietf-tls-esni no RFC number at revision 25 -- and the
text publishes no test vectors. So the evidence is live ECHConfigList values
pulled from real HTTPS resource records, two-sided round-trip, and each of
s5.1's four MUST-aborts asserted by its own reason.

**ECH offered from tls.client**, and **two live servers accept it**:
crypto.cloudflare.com, and defo.ie -- a different operator publishing three
configs in one list. Both decrypted a ClientHelloInner this client encrypted,
used it, and signalled acceptance in ServerHello.random, which the client
recomputed from its own inner transcript and matched. That is an independent
implementation agreeing, and for ECH it is the only external evidence there
is. The control: flipping one bit of the config public key gets :ech-rejected
from both.

The inner hello is padded by s6.1.3's scheme. Without it the ciphertext length
tracks the inner SNI length -- the one thing ECH exists to hide -- so an
unpadded client would advertise the length of the name it was hiding.

**ECH is complete.** s6.1.6's rejection recovery landed too: the client
finishes the handshake against the public_name, collects retry_configs from
EncryptedExtensions, sends ech_required and returns an error rather than a
connection -- because s6.1.7 says authenticating for the public name does not
authenticate the origin. Measured against Cloudflare, which hands back a retry
config with a different config_id from the one its DNS record publishes.

ECH's HelloRetryRequest path stays unreachable rather than omitted: this
client refuses HelloRetryRequest by name, so s7.2.1 cannot arise from it.

**MLS signs, on five of seven suites.** Ed25519 for 1 and 3, ECDSA over
P-256/384/521 for 2, 5 and 7 -- the latter built into org-nist-ecc against
1,530 Wycheproof verification cases, 930 of them invalid, rejected across
sixteen distinct reasons. Ed448 (suites 4 and 6) refuses by name, and a test
asserts that rather than skipping those suites.

**The MLS key schedule**, checked as a chain: 7 suites by 5 epochs, run
forward from the initial init secret, never reading an epoch's init secret out
of the fixture to feed the next. 17,340 assertions on both runtimes.

RFC 9420's Figure 22 states its convention in prose above the diagram, and it
is the one thing there that is easy to invert: Extract takes its salt from the
top and its IKM from the left. Inverting it gives a key schedule that is
entirely self-consistent -- every member running the same wrong code agrees
with every other -- and shares nothing with anyone else. There is no way to
catch that from inside a group. It is what an interop corpus is for, and it is
the same shape as the two-sided-test finding above, one level up: a whole
implementation agreeing with itself is not evidence either.

**The MLS secret tree** -- RFC 9420 §9 and §6.3.2. 21 trees, 287 leaves, both
ratchets, generations 0 and 15. 20,892 assertions on both runtimes.

Two of its breaks are worth carrying. Siblings in the secret tree are told
apart by the **context**, not the label: both children derive with the label
tree, and what differs is left versus right. Swapping the roles of label and
context makes siblings equal, so every member of the group would hold every
other member's keys -- 2,240 red either way, and identical in the summary
line to swapping left and right themselves.

And generation 15 is why a corpus with two entries beats one with one. A
ratchet is a hash chain, so an implementation that used the generation only as
a context -- deriving generation 15 straight from the root -- agrees at
generation 0 and diverges everywhere after. A corpus that published only
generation 0 would have called it correct.

**PSK secrets and transcript hashes** -- RFC 9420 §8.4 and §8.2, the key
schedule's two remaining inputs. 21,114 assertions on both runtimes, against
77 PSK combinations and 7 transcript suites.

One technique there is worth keeping. A Commit's AuthenticatedContent is the
confirmed-hash input followed by an opaque confirmation tag, and **the
boundary between them is not marked** -- finding it by parsing means parsing a
whole Commit. It does not have to be parsed: the tag is recomputable, so its
encoded form is a known suffix, and matching it finds the boundary and
verifies the tag in one step. That is stronger than parsing would have been,
and it let a layer land before the layer underneath it.

### A justification can be wrong in a way the code is not

MLS-Exporter's label in the corpus is a 64-character string of hex digits. It
was read as hex, and on the strength of that reading the labelled-KDF helper
was generalised to accept raw bytes as well as strings -- with a docstring
explaining why the generalisation was necessary and citing the vectors.

It is a label that happens to look like hex, passed as text. The generalisation
ran fine; it was needed by nothing; and the docstring asserted a fact about the
corpus that was false. The code was harmless and the prose was not, because the
prose is what the next reader would have believed.

That is the third member of a family this ADR keeps finding: a justification
written next to a branch is not evidence about that branch; a round-trip is not
evidence about what both ends compute the same way; and now, a generalisation
is not evidence that the case it generalises for exists.

### The wrong answer came from our own documentation

The previous revision of this ADR said this workspace had no portable
signature, and made it a numbered prerequisite blocking all of MLS. It was
false. org-ietf-ed25519's edwards.cljc and scalar.cljc have **zero** reader
conditionals, and its ClojureScript smoke test asserts signatures
byte-identical to the JVM's. It had been portable for some time.

The claim was taken from CLAUDE.md, which listed that repository among the
JVM-only libraries. That sentence was true when written and stopped being true
without changing -- the same shape as org-nist-sha2's claim that SHA-384 and
SHA-512 were absent, three headings above the section documenting them.

This is the fifth absence claim in this program that turned out to be false,
and the first where the wrong answer came from **reading this workspace's own
prose instead of the repository**. The rule about searching before concluding
absence is written against not looking; this was looking, in the wrong place,
at something that looked authoritative. CLAUDE.md now says so at the point
where the list is, and says to count reader conditionals instead of quoting
it.

### Documenting a trap is not removing it

The public_name check of s6.1.7 was wrong twice, the same way both times. It
takes a byte vector, and both wrong versions reached for characters: first a
regex with one escape too many, so no name split and every valid name was
rejected; then (map int over a string), which is code points on the JVM and
zeros under ClojureScript. Then the test's own helper did the second one too.

That second trap is documented in hpke.kdf/ascii, in this workspace, by us --
and it was walked into three times in one afternoon by code written to be
careful about it. The fix was not more care: the check compares bytes to byte
constants now and never sees a character, which has neither failure available
to it.

### Only the live handshake noticed

The transcript starts with whichever ClientHello the server used, and that
decision was invisible to the entire unit suite. Forcing it to always pick the
outer -- which gives a client that builds both hellos correctly, encrypts
correctly, judges acceptance correctly, and then derives its keys from the
wrong transcript -- left every test green.

It is a named function with a test now. That is the third time this week the
same remedy applied: org-nist-ecc's unreachable doubling branch, HPKE's
unreachable rejection-sampling condition, and this. **A decision worth
getting right is worth a name, because a decision buried in an expression is
a decision no test can address.**

**org-ietf-mls** -- RFC 9420's Appendix C tree arithmetic and the labelled
operations of s5.1.3, s5.2 and s8. HPKE's third consumer. 16,510 assertions on
both runtimes, against the working group's own corpus rather than a vendor's:
tree-math's 10 trees from 1 to 512 leaves, and crypto-basics' seven suites.

All seven cipher suites run -- and the signature half of none of them does.
Every MLS suite names Ed25519, Ed448 or ECDSA, and this workspace has none of
them portably. The suites are still listed, because their KEM, KDF and AEAD
halves are what the key schedule and TreeKEM are built from, and the README
says which half seven refers to.

The nulls in tree-math are the interesting part: a leaf has no children and
the root has no parent, and an implementation returning 0 or the node itself
would agree with the corpus everywhere else.

**And a break that hangs is a break nobody can measure.** Breaking `parent`
made `direct-path` loop forever rather than fail. The loop is bounded now -- a
path cannot be longer than the tree -- so a wrong `parent` produces an error
instead of running out the clock. That bound is not defence against a hostile
input; index arithmetic cannot be attacked. It exists so the failure is
observable.

### A two-sided test cannot check a value both sides agree on

The finding from ECH, and it generalises past ECH. Both halves live in one
namespace, so a **symmetric** change to the HPKE info left every round-trip
test green. Dropping the ECHConfig from the info -- which makes the context
wrong against any real peer -- broke **nothing at all** until the value was
pulled into its own function and pinned to the draft's text rather than to
the other half of the code.

That is a third member of the family this ADR already records. A corpus can
be complete and not cover what you assume; a justification written next to a
branch is not evidence about that branch; and a round-trip is not evidence
about anything the two ends compute the same way.

Two bugs surfaced on the way, both in shapes CLAUDE.md warns about.
tls.result/error merges the caller's context map **over** its own
:tls/alert and :tls/reason, so passing :tls/reason in the context silently
replaced the stable keyword -- invisible to any test asserting that an error
happened rather than which one. And a test's expected value used (map int
over a string), which is code points on the JVM and a vector of zeros under
ClojureScript: the trap hpke.kdf/ascii documents, walked into by a test
written to check exactly that class of thing.

**org-ietf-odoh** -- RFC 9230, HPKE's first consumer here. 229 assertions on
both runtimes. The response is not a second encapsulation: the target exports
an AEAD key from the same HPKE context, salted with the query plaintext, so
the client must keep that plaintext and the response is bound to those exact
query bytes. RFC 9230 publishes no vectors; the corpus is cloudflare/odoh-go's
and the tests are named for what it is.

### Three findings from those two, worth more than the code

**A corpus can be complete and still not cover the thing you assume it
covers.** Wycheproof supplies both parties' keys, so nothing in its 1,806
ECDH cases multiplies the base point: gx and gy could both be wrong and all
of them would pass. Measured -- one wrong digit in P-256's gy leaves the
whole corpus green and turns four assertions red, all of them in the
parameter invariants and the java.security oracle.

**A justification written next to a branch is not evidence about that
branch.** org-nist-ecc's bignum wrapper was first justified by the doubling
discriminant inside add-points. That branch turns out to be unreachable from
scalar-mult -- left-to-right double-and-add adds P to a multiple of P that is
never P -- so forcing it false left the entire suite green. The wrapper is
necessary, but for a different branch (Z = 0 is the point at infinity, and
ClojureScript's zero? answers false for a BigInt zero: 1,767 red). The
sentence would have stood unchallenged because nothing tested what it named.

**A break that is not a break reads exactly like a missing test.** In
org-ietf-x448, an attempted break masked bit 7 off before setting it --
`(bit-or (bit-and x 0x7F) 0x80)`, which is the identity. Zero failures. The
finding looked like \"the clamp test does not discriminate\" and was actually
\"the break was not one\". CLAUDE.md already warns about the mirror image of
this (a red produced by breaking the wrong thing); this is the same error
producing a green.

## The MLS framing layer landed (2026-08-27)

mls.proposal, mls.framing and mls.message: FramedContent, Sender,
FramedContentAuthData, AuthenticatedContent, PublicMessage,
PrivateMessage, MLSMessage, and the Proposal/Commit codecs framing cannot
work without. Against the working group's message-protection corpus --
seven suites, each with a proposal, a commit and an application message
in both wire formats. 61 tests / 21,358 assertions on both runtimes.

Both directions are exercised and they are labelled apart. Unprotecting
what another implementation protected is the check that means something;
protecting content and unprotecting it again is a round-trip, and a
round-trip is only evidence that one implementation is self-consistent.

Three guarantees sit underneath and a PublicMessage has two of them: the
signature over FramedContentTBS says who wrote it, the membership tag
over AuthenticatedContentTBM says the writer is in this group in this
epoch, and encryption says nobody outside the group can read it. The tag
is checked before the signature -- it is symmetric, so it rejects most
garbage before any public-key work happens.

Two findings worth keeping:

**A parser needed a parser.** FramedContent embeds a Proposal or a Commit
with no length prefix, so a reader that cannot say where a Commit ends
cannot say where the signature after it begins. Only application content
(an opaque<V>) is self-delimiting. This is why mls.proposal had to exist
before framing could -- and why the transcript-hash layer, which landed
first, had to reach the same content by recomputing the confirmation tag
and matching it as a known suffix instead.

**Refusing by name is not the same as not implementing.** add and update
proposals, and a Commit's UpdatePath, carry KeyPackage and LeafNode --
ratchet-tree encodings that do not exist yet. They return
:unimplemented-proposal-type and :unimplemented-update-path rather than
falling through the dispatch. This parser does not do that yet and that
is not a proposal type are different things to tell a caller, and only
the first one tells the caller to wait.

Suites 4 and 6 sign with Ed448, which this workspace does not have. They
are not skipped: their messages are decoded, their content codecs
checked, and the signature step asserted to refuse by that name. A
skipped suite and a passing suite look identical in a summary line.
Application data may not travel as a PublicMessage (RFC 9420 6.1), and
that refusal is placed before the signature step so it holds for those
two suites as well -- a rule that depended on being able to sign would be
no rule at all for the suites that cannot.

Discrimination measured by breaking one thing at a time:

  FramedContentTBS omits the GroupContext      31 red
  the reuse guard is not applied               23 red
  one ratchet for every content type           15 red
  application data allowed in a PublicMessage   7 red
  the membership tag is not checked             2 red
  the private-message padding is not checked    1 red
  restored                                       0

## The ratchet tree landed (2026-08-27)

mls.node, mls.tree-hash and mls.tree-ops: the node encodings, the tree hash
and the resolution, and the three proposals that change the tree's shape.
With those, the add and update proposals and a Commit's UpdatePath -- which
refused by name -- go through. 76 tests / 47,141 assertions on both runtimes,
against three more of the working group's corpora.

**A codec corpus is not a semantic corpus.** The messages corpus is 300
entries of structs another implementation serialised with random field
values; nothing in it is cryptographically valid and none of it is meant to
be. Decoding those bytes and re-encoding to the same bytes is agreement about
the wire format and nothing more -- which is exactly the question a wire
format raises and exactly not the question a tree raises. tree-validation, 98
real trees with every node's hash and resolution, is a separate corpus and a
separate set of tests, and the two counts are not added together.

Four findings:

**The corpus can miss the step that makes an operation distinctive.** Add is
the only one of the three shape-changing proposals that does NOT blank the
direct path -- it records the new leaf as unmerged instead. The working
group's tree-operations corpus has two Add cases and neither reaches that
step: one extends into an all-blank subtree, the other's blank leaf has no
non-blank ancestor. Measured, before writing anything else: an implementation
that blanks the path on Add passes that corpus with nothing red. The states
had to come from tree-validation, which holds 63 trees where the leftmost
blank leaf does sit under a non-blank ancestor -- and even there, the
MUST-be-sorted rule for unmerged_leaves has no state anywhere in the corpus
that tells the two orderings apart. That one case is constructed and labelled
as constructed. **A passing corpus is evidence about what the corpus reaches,
and finding out what it reaches is a separate measurement.**

**A range check needs its accepting side tested too.** Epochs now go through
a uint64 reader that refuses anything outside the range both runtimes hold
exactly. The byte offsets in that check were off by two, so it would have
refused valid epochs -- and a test that only asserted the too-big direction
would have passed. It was caught by asserting the boundary from below as
well. This is the mirror of the rule already in this workspace's CLAUDE.md
about negative tests that pass for the wrong reason: **an over-strict check
and a correct one produce the same output on the input a refusal test uses.**

**A parser that throws is worse than one that refuses, and the corpus found
it immediately.** Lifetime.not_after is routinely 2^64-1, which is what
implementations actually put there. Reading it into a JVM long raises an
uncaught ArithmeticException; reading it into a JavaScript number silently
drops the low bits. The same read was reachable from decode-private-message
with twelve bytes of peer-supplied input. Lifetime now keeps its two fields as
eight-byte vectors -- nothing compares them, because the lifetime check is not
implemented, and a number that cannot be trusted is a worse foundation for
that check than bytes that can.

**The workspace's own prose was wrong again, in the same shape as before.**
mls.suite's docstring said no MLS suite here can sign, because ed25519 was
JVM-only and there was no ECDSA. Both had stopped being true earlier in this
same programme. It has been corrected in place, with the correction left
visible: five of seven suites sign, Ed448 is the gap. This is the second time
in this programme that a confident sentence about another repository's
capability outlived the capability question it answered -- the first was
CLAUDE.md's JVM-only list. Prose about what another repository can do goes
stale silently and goes stale with an authoritative tone.

Discrimination measured by breaking one thing at a time:

  the leaf hash input uses the node index          3095 red
  LeafNodeTBS omits the group id and leaf index     721 red
  Add blanks the direct path                        169 red
  the ratchet_tree is not extended after decoding   102 red
  it is extended to odd, not to a power of two      102 red
  trailing blanks are kept when encoding             51 red
  the resolution lists unmerged by leaf index        21 red
  Update does not blank the path                      2 red
  Remove does not truncate the tree                   2 red
  unmerged_leaves is appended to, not sorted into     2 red
  the uint64 range check reads only the first byte    1 red
  restored                                            0

## What remains, in dependency order

  1. MLS parent hashes       What makes a tree authenticated rather than
                             merely agreed on: the tree hash says the group
                             holds the same tree, the parent hash says each
                             node's key was put there by someone entitled to.
                             Everything it needs is now here.
  2. MLS, after that         TreeKEM and Welcome.
  3. Ed448                   MLS suites 4 and 6 name it and nothing here
                             implements it. The other five suites sign, so
                             this is not a prerequisite for MLS -- it is two
                             of seven suites.

## What this does not claim

Nothing here is deployed. HPKE is a leaf library with no consumer in this
workspace, which is the honest reading of \"implemented\": the primitive is
real and the things RFC 9180 was written for are not built yet.

The BouncyCastle oracle still covers base mode only. A second opinion on
base mode is not a second opinion on auth mode, and the README says so
rather than letting \"differential tested\" spread across all four.")))
